  "worked_solution": "First, we let $f(x)=3{x}^{2}-6x+51.$\n\nNext, we solve $f(x)=0$ by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-6x+51 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+17 & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1\\cdot x+{1}^{2})-{1}^{2}+17 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}-1+17 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+16 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =-16 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =16{\\text{i}}^{2} \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =(4\\text{i})^{2} \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ x & =1\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since our quadratic equation has roots ${x}_{1}=1+4\\text{i}$ and ${x}_{2}=1-4\\text{i},$ and the coefficient of the quadratic term is $a=3,$ the expression $3{x}^{2}-6x+51$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-6x+51 & =a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2}) \\\\  & =3(x-(1+4\\text{i}))(x-(1-4\\text{i})).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
